From 1ab668d51e4a809bcb11c11ac0f25c79fff8c6a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mrfelek <17274927+mrfelek@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:37:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(agc): reuse data-only hit stages whose buffer bases
 moved

A data-only hit compiled a stage again whenever a used user word
differed, even when the word is only a buffer base and the data behind
the new base equals the cached runs. Such stages now reuse the cached
variant with its runs shifted, validated by content, and forwarded
words patched. Stages whose vertex fetch decode reads guest memory
become relocatable when every read names its user word. A stage whose
differing words are only values forwarded to the shader, and held by
no buffer or image descriptor, reuses the variant with those values
patched. A value that is the base of a guest buffer descriptor is
patched into that descriptor too, as is a buffer base a short offset
above it, unless the base sits in captured memory or another user word
may have produced it. A forwarded word the shader also reads
memory through is relocated like a base. Moves that would change how a
fresh capture splits or orders its runs are refused.

APS5_NO_DATA_HIT_RELOCATION, APS5_NO_VERTEX_FETCH_RELOCATION,
APS5_NO_DATA_HIT_FORWARDED_PATCH, APS5_NO_DATA_HIT_BUFFER_BASE_PATCH
and APS5_NO_DATA_HIT_VALUE_BASE turn the parts off. The draw-cache
trace counts relocated stages, verified stages that moved, and why
cached stages were passed over. The data-hit verifier also compares
bindings and specialization, and names the first differing binding and the words a moved stage
was moved by.

AI-assisted. Tested by me on my own machine.
